A||||6||mshill||Suspension Techniques springs at JC Whitney...||||1||
Z||000000||mshill||01-17-2000||02:43 PM||||The JC Whitney catalog shows some Suspension Techniques "High Performance - Lowering Sport Springs" for $80 front ro rear.  <P>Does anyone know how these compare to the Eibach sport lowering springs that the Fiero Store sells for about $45 more for the pair( $205 compared to $160)?<P>I have the Eibach springs on my 86GT, but I think they lower the car about 2 inches, which is a little too much.  The JCW catalog says the ST springs will lower a car 1-1.5 inches.   I am looking to put these on my 86SE that is getting new struts and a fresh engine.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000001||87PegasusGT||01-17-2000||03:16 PM||||I am also wondering about the swaybars with shocks & struts that JC sells.||||reg||1
Z||000002||batboy||01-17-2000||03:22 PM||||I installed those Suspension Techniques Sport Lowering Springs on the Batmobile (85 SE) last fall. I had lots of extra room in the front wheel wells to begin with. I had to go with a smaller front tire afterwards, so my total amount lowered was close to 2 inches. In my case it turned out good, but with many Fieros, this is too much to drop. So far, I've been pleased with the ride and handling, but I have yet to push it hard because I still have to get an alignment. Doesn't anyone make a spring with only a 1 inch drop? Coil-overs of course, if your budget can afford it...<p>[This message has been edited by batboy (edited 01-17-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||1FST2M6||01-17-2000||03:39 PM||||I worked at NOPI for about a year and ST stuff if great. They stand behind all their products like gold! Eibach pro-kits springs are only a 1.3" drop and 35% stiffer. not too bad a ride quality, just becareful if you get a set of Konis if you don't set them right you'll hate the ride and blame it on the springs... The KYBs are a great way to go decent sporty feel without the hassel of fine tuning all four corners. KYBs and ST springs that would be a good combo! KYBs can handle the lowered stance of the car and not blow out like a gabriel or monroe.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Spyder||01-17-2000||03:54 PM||||I have installed the JC Whitney sway bars which are addco sway bars, and the suspension techniques springs, and here is a pic.  <A HREF="http://www.mindspring.com/~mm889-19/suspension.jpg" TARGET=_blank>http://www.mindspring.com/~mm889-19/suspension.jpg</A> <BR>I’ve also added the poly bushings, which all this together makes it ride like a race car, which I like.<BR>If you want you can cut your own springs to the height you want. I was told to cut a half a coil at a time.||||reg||1
Z||000005||mshill||01-17-2000||04:26 PM||||batboy,  my 86GT has Eibach springs and 15 inch wheels with 60 series tires which makes it 2 inches lower than my 86SE, one-half inch of which is in the wheel tire combo.<P>On my 86SE I am keeping the stock wheels and tires.  Hope there won't be any rubbing problems.||||reg||1
Z||000006||2birds||01-18-2000||09:34 PM||||I JC Whitney-ized my 86 GT 5 years ago, with the springs and swaybars.  I got almost a 2" drop on mine, and its definitely in the weeds.  I kept the Monroe Gas-matic shocks and replaced all the ball joints and put in the poly bushings.  Spyder is 100% right about the ride being a little rough.  The ST springs are linear rate, but I think the Eibachs are variable rate.  I think the variables would be worth the extra bucks if you have a lot of rough roads where you live.  The poly bushings don't make it any less harsh, either.  It is a worthwile trade off, but I have the wiggle hip feel at the rear end during pavement transitions, possibly rear bump steer.  Supposedly this is common on lowered Fieros.  I never replaced any of the tie rod ends, so I'll probably do that before I try the bump steer eliminators, since I have the uneven tracking at high speeds that was described in another post.  As far as clearance goes, the tires look right now,with 215/60/15 in front and 225/60/15 in back on stock rims, it will rub the front side of the front inner fender liner at full lock.  This only happens in a parking lot, anyway, so no big deal.  Alignment rack and floor jacks are fun, too.  <BR>Has anybody seen a Fiero with airbag springs?  I've seen them in the street rod and Honda mags, and I wonder if they would be worth the effort.<BR>||||reg||1
